add(1,'Requested dense expositions/code on modular symbols','Modular symbols begin with a boundary relation','Modular forms',
 'What does a modular symbol encode, and which relations can be proved before invoking a modular-form computation?',
 'The recovered request sought direct mathematics on modular symbols. The degree-zero cusp-divisor presentation supplies an exact starting point. It explains the triangle relation and the role of a base cusp, while keeping numerical integration and congruence-group equivariance as further requirements.',
 ['For S=P¹(Q), let Δ₀=ker[Z[S]→Z], where the map sums coefficients. Let M be generated by {a,b}, with {a,a}=0 and {a,b}+{b,c}={a,c}. A weight-k modular symbol additionally uses a declared group action and coefficient module V_{k−2}.'],
 'Triangle presentation of cusp divisors',
 'The map {a,b}↦[b]−[a] identifies M with Δ₀. In particular {a,b}=−{b,a}, and every symbol is a difference of symbols based at one cusp.',
 'The relations map to zero by telescoping. Fix o∈S and send a degree-zero divisor Σn_a[a] to Σn_a{o,a}. The triangle relation gives {a,b}={o,b}−{o,a}, so this is inverse on each generator. Conversely Σn_a([a]−[o])=Σn_a[a] because Σn_a=0. Thus both injectivity and surjectivity are proved. Equivariance can subsequently be imposed relative to a specified congruence subgroup; it does not follow from arbitrary cusp labels or a randomly populated matrix. Integration of an actual differential must likewise respect paths and convergence at cusps.',
 'Use o=∞ and cusps0,1,∞.',
 '{∞,0}+{0,1}+{1,∞}=0 maps to([0]−[∞])+([1]−[0])+([∞]−[1])=0. Therefore {0,1}={∞,1}−{∞,0}. This exact relation is checkable without numerical periods.',
 'The algebra identifies a genuine presentation rather than a theorem-shaped software record. A valid implementation must state which further quotient, coefficient representation and Hecke operators it computes.',
 'This does not compute a cusp-form space or period lattice. A formal triangle relation alone does not certify the mocked modular-symbol routines mentioned in the historical code.',
 'Fix Γ₀(N), its coefficient representation and Manin relations, then compute the resulting finite module with independently checked Hecke action.',[EC])
add(2,'Hecke recurrence','Local Euler factors distinguish three coefficient sequences','Elliptic L functions',
 'How does a good-prime point count determine Euler coefficients, and how do these differ from Frobenius power sums?',
 'The July8 material connected modular forms, Galois representations and elliptic curves. The precise elementary bridge is a formal reciprocal quadratic. Once the local trace is declared, it gives an exact recurrence without proving the deeper modularity or representation-theoretic theorems.',
 ['At a good prime p put a_p=p+1−#E(F_p), and define A(T)=Σ_{r≥0}a_{p^r}T^r=(1−a_pT+pT²)⁻¹. Its constant coefficient is1. All coefficient calculations take place formally, so analytic convergence is irrelevant.'],
 'Good-prime recurrence',
 'For r≥2, a_{p^r}=a_pa_{p^{r−1}}−pa_{p^{r−2}}. If α,β solve X²−a_pX+p=0, then a_{p^r}=Σ_{j=0}^rα^jβ^{r−j}, whereas the Frobenius power sum is α^r+β^r.',
 'Multiplying A by its denominator and comparing coefficients gives the recurrence and uniquely determines its formal inverse. Alternatively expand(1−αT)⁻¹(1−βT)⁻¹ and collect degree r. In particular a_{p²}=a_p²−p, but α²+β²=a_p²−2p. The extension-field count uses the latter: #E(F_{p²})=p²+1−(α²+β²). This distinction prevents an Euler-series coefficient from being silently substituted for a point-count trace. For a weight-k eigenform with character χ the determinant term instead is χ(p)p^{k−1}.',
 'Take E:y²=x³−x at p=5.',
 'For x=0,1,2,3,4 the y-counts are1,1,2,2,1. Infinity makes eight points, so a₅=−2. The local reciprocal has coefficients1,−2,−1,12,−19 through degree4. The extension-field formula gives #E(F₂₅)=26−(4−10)=32, while the Euler coefficient a₂₅ is−1.',
 'An exact short calculation now separates local traces, prime-power Euler coefficients and extension-field counts.',
 'Bad primes require different local factors. Matching a finite recurrence does not construct a Galois representation, verify an Euler system or prove BSD.',
 'Enumerate several finite extensions for a named good prime and verify the trace relation separately from the formal Euler recurrence.',[EC])
add(3,'Weierstrass equation and Tate invariants','Weierstrass invariants are a first step toward Tate data','Elliptic arithmetic',
 'Which exact invariants certify smoothness and good reduction, and what still requires a local reduction algorithm?',
 'The historical request included Tate’s algorithm and conductor data. A discriminant calculation is substantial but does not supply all of that algorithm. A complete example identifies good primes and a singular-prime gap without confusing a model invariant with a conductor exponent.',
 ['For y²+a₁xy+a₃y=x³+a₂x²+a₄x+a₆, set b₂=a₁²+4a₂, b₄=2a₄+a₁a₃, b₆=a₃²+4a₆ and b₈=a₁²a₆+4a₂a₆−a₁a₃a₄+a₂a₃²−a₄². Set c₄=b₂²−24b₄ and c₆=−b₂³+36b₂b₄−216b₆.'],
 'Exact discriminant identity',
 'Δ=−b₂²b₈−8b₄³−27b₆²+9b₂b₄b₆ satisfies c₄³−c₆²=1728Δ. The Weierstrass cubic is smooth over a field exactly when its discriminant is nonzero there.',
 'Substitution proves the polynomial invariant identity. The discriminant condition results by eliminating common zeros of the cubic and its partial derivatives. In characteristic different from two and three, for y²=x³+Ax+B this reduces to Δ=−16(4A³+27B²): a repeated root r satisfies3r²+A=0 and r³+Ar+B=0, yielding4A³+27B²=0. An integral model with p-adic unit discriminant therefore has smooth reduction. A divisible discriminant still leaves minimality and reduction type to be checked, especially at small primes. Its valuation cannot simply be labeled the conductor exponent.',
 'Use E:y²=x³−x.',
 'Here b₂=0,b₄=−2,b₆=0,b₈=−1,c₄=48,c₆=0 and Δ=64. Thus j=48³/64=1728 and every odd prime is a good-reduction prime for this model. The identity48³=1728·64 holds exactly. The prime two remains exceptional and is not resolved by this calculation.',
 'The result gives a reproducible smoothness certificate with explicit limits on what a partial Tate routine can claim.',
 'No full Tate-algorithm branch at two is executed here. Coordinate changes can multiply a model discriminant by twelfth powers, making minimality essential.',
 'Return a certificate for each local coordinate change, residual polynomial and branch condition before reporting minimal discriminant and conductor data.',[EC])
add(4,'Selmer triangle and','Selmer bounds require actual local conditions','Galois cohomology',
 'What exact sequence relates rational points, the n-Selmer group and the Tate–Shafarevich group?',
 'The historical skeleton used simplified cocycles rather than verified Euler-system arithmetic. The Kummer sequence states the genuine object a descent computation must certify and explains exactly why Selmer dimension is a rank bound rather than automatically the rank.',
 ['Let E/K be an elliptic curve over a number field and n≥2. Define Ш(E/K)=ker[H¹(K,E)→∏_vH¹(K_v,E)]. Sel_n consists of H¹(K,E[n]) classes whose image in every H¹(K_v,E) is zero.'],
 'Kummer–Selmer exact sequence',
 'There is an exact sequence0→E(K)/nE(K)→Sel_n(E/K)→Ш(E/K)[n]→0. For n=p prime, if the Selmer dimension is s and dim_Fp E(K)[p]=t, then rank E(K)≤s−t.',
 'Multiplication by n on E over an algebraic closure is surjective with kernel E[n]. Its cohomology sequence gives0→E(K)/nE(K)→H¹(K,E[n])→H¹(K,E)[n]→0, and the analogous local sequences hold. Taking preimages of locally trivial global classes proves the displayed sequence: every n-torsion class of Ш has a Kummer lift, and each local image of that lift is locally Kummer. Finite generation writes E(K)=Z^r⊕T, so E(K)/pE(K) has dimension r+t. Its injection into Sel_p yields the inequality. Equality requires ruling out the remaining Ш[p] contribution; a norm-square condition by itself need not enforce every local condition.',
 'Suppose independently checked certificate data give s=4 and t=1.',
 'Then r≤3. Three rational points with a positive-definite height Gram matrix give r≥3, forcing rank three. With only two independent points known, rank two and rank three remain possible because Ш[p] may account for a Selmer dimension.',
 'An actual rank proof needs both a valid local-condition upper bound and a certified independence lower bound.',
 'These certificate dimensions are stipulated, not a recovered computation for a named curve. Mock cocycles and an FFI theorem name do not prove Selmer membership.',
 'Specify an explicit descent map and every relevant local field; return exact local witnesses and the finite linear algebra computing the Selmer subgroup.',[EC])
add(5,'height-pairing refinement','Height pairings turn independence into a Gram certificate','Arithmetic lattices',
 'How can canonical-height data certify independent points, and how does subgroup index affect a regulator?',
 'The request for height refinement and BSD quantities becomes precise once a quadratic height and free subgroup are fixed. A Gram determinant can certify independence, but saturation is a separate issue and changes the regulator by a square factor.',
 ['Let q be canonical height modulo torsion, with q(nP)=n²q(P) and the parallelogram law. Define B(P,Q)=[q(P+Q)−q(P)−q(Q)]/2, so B(P,P)=q(P). For points P_i put G_ij=B(P_i,P_j).'],
 'Positive height Gram matrices and index',
 'Positive definiteness of G implies the P_i are independent modulo torsion. If their subgroup has finite index I in the full free Mordell–Weil lattice, detG is I² times the full regulator in this convention.',
 'The quadratic-height identities give bilinearity and q(Σc_iP_i)=cᵀGc. A torsion combination has height zero, whereas a positive-definite matrix gives a positive value whenever the integer vector c is nonzero. For the index formula express the P_i in a full lattice basis using an integer matrix C. Then G_P=CᵀG_fullC, so detG_P=(detC)²detG_full. The absolute determinant is the lattice index. The same equation shows why a numerically positive matrix does not alone certify a full regulator: it first needs rigorous error control and then an independent saturation argument.',
 'Use the stipulated Gram matrix[[2,1],[1,3]].',
 'Its principal minors are2 and5. Its quadratic form is2a²+2ab+3b²=2(a+b/2)²+(5/2)b², positive for any nonzero pair. Hence the two points would be independent and their subgroup regulator would be5. If the subgroup index were two, the full regulator would instead be5/4.',
 'The proof explains which arithmetic conclusion a positive height certificate supports and exactly which information it leaves open.',
 'No specific elliptic points or verified height intervals were recovered for this example. A regulator is only one factor in a BSD leading term and does not prove that conjecture.',
 'Certify height intervals preserving positive definiteness and prove saturation at all candidate index primes before reporting the full regulator.',[EC])
add(6,'ABHN','Quaternion invariants obey a global reciprocity constraint','Brauer groups',
 'Which local data define a global Brauer class, and what is checked by the invariant sum?',
 'The proposed Lean structures named cyclic algebras and local invariants but did not perform their arithmetic. Hamilton’s quaternion algebra provides genuine local data and an exact reciprocity check, separating an interface schema from a local splitting proof.',
 ['The quaternion algebra(a,b)_K has generators i²=a,j²=b,ij=−ji. A local split quaternion algebra has Brauer invariant0 and a division quaternion algebra has invariant1/2. The Albert–Brauer–Hasse–Noether theorem identifies global classes with finitely supported local invariant families summing to zero in Q/Z.'],
 'Two ramified places for Hamilton’s algebra',
 'For H=(−1,−1)_Q, the invariants are1/2 at2 and∞ and zero at every odd prime. Thus their sum is zero modulo one, while H itself is nontrivial.',
 'Over R its norm is a sum of four squares, anisotropic on nonzero vectors, so H is division. Over Q₂ a putative isotropic vector can be scaled to a primitive integral quadruple. Squares are0,1,4 modulo eight. If the number of odd coordinates is1,2,3 or4, their sum with the even squares cannot be0 modulo eight; hence the norm remains anisotropic. At an odd prime the local Hilbert-symbol formula for unit parameters gives(−1,−1)_p=1, hence splitting. The two nonsplit invariants sum to1=0 in Q/Z. The cited global injection detects the nontrivial class from either localization. This example checks the reciprocity constraint, rather than proving the full theorem from one case.',
 'Compare the actual family{2↦1/2,∞↦1/2} with a proposed family{∞↦1/2}.',
 'The first has total0 modulo one and occurs for H. The second totals1/2 and cannot be the complete localization list of any global central simple algebra. Individually valid local numbers therefore need not be globally compatible.',
 'A correct checker needs both justified local calculations and the global compatibility condition.',
 'This does not compute a Brauer–Manin set. That requires evaluating specified classes at adelic points, controlling all relevant places and proving those evaluations.',
 'For a concrete cyclic algebra, return valuation and residue certificates at every possible ramified place, then verify reciprocity before forming an obstruction pairing.',[CFT])
add(7,'certificate records','Exact certificates can cross an untrusted solver boundary','Proof certificates',
 'Which witness lets a small checker verify an optimization or rank answer returned by external code?',
 'The historical pipeline mixed Lean, opaque foreign pointers and arithmetic engines. A binding alone is not a proof. Exact linear certificates illustrate how complex untrusted computation can propose data whose decisive properties are checked with elementary mathematics.',
 ['For min cᵀx subject to Ax≥b,x≥0, the dual is max bᵀy subject to Aᵀy≤c,y≥0. A rank-r witness can pair a nonzero r×r minor with an exact factorization A=UV through an r-dimensional space.'],
 'Matching primal and dual values certify optimality',
 'Feasible x,y with cᵀx=bᵀy are optimal. A matrix with a nonzero r-minor and a factorization through dimension r has exact rank r.',
 'For any feasible pair, bᵀy≤(Ax)ᵀy=xᵀAᵀy≤cᵀx by nonnegativity. Equality at one pair excludes a smaller primal value and a larger dual value. For rank, a nonsingular r-minor gives r independent columns, while A=UV bounds the image dimension by r. These proofs use only exact equalities, inequalities and dimensions. The search procedure may be complicated, but its correctness is unnecessary once the complete witness is independently checked. Conversely a solver’s status string, foreign pointer or unproved theorem declaration is not such a witness.',
 'Minimize x+2y with x+y≥3 and x,y≥0.',
 'The primal point(3,0) has value3. The dual scalar λ=1 satisfies λ≤1,λ≤2 and has the same value3. Therefore the optimum is exactly3. A floating answer2.999999 needs a feasibility/error certificate rather than being silently rounded into a theorem.',
 'A meaningful mathematical API returns the witness and every hypothesis used by its checker.',
 'A bounded model check proves only its finite domain. This linear certificate does not validate a nonlinear model or an elliptic-rank answer lacking genuine local and height hypotheses.',
 'Specify exact rational reconstruction and certificate dimensions; measure checker soundness separately from search success.',[('Boyd and Vandenberghe, Convex Optimization','https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/cvxbook/')])
add(8,'CRDT/vector-clock consistency','Vector clocks describe a partial order with a join','Order theory',
 'Which merge law makes duplicate and reordered messages harmless, and what remains undecided for concurrent events?',
 'The platform discussion named operation DAGs and vector clocks. Their mathematical core is a join semilattice. A finite model proves the merge law while showing why it neither orders all events nor establishes arbitrary payload consistency.',
 ['Order N^d componentwise and set(x∨y)_i=max(x_i,y_i). Under a vector-clock discipline, a replica merges observed clocks and increments its own coordinate for a new local event. Incomparable clocks denote concurrency in that declared model.'],
 'Componentwise maximum is a least upper bound',
 'The merge ∨ is associative, commutative and idempotent, and is the least upper bound in the componentwise order. Every finite multiset of received clocks therefore has an order-independent merged value, unchanged by repetition.',
 'Each identity reduces coordinatewise to the corresponding elementary maximum identity. If z bounds x and y, every z_i≥max(x_i,y_i), proving leastness. Repeated associativity and commutativity remove message ordering; idempotence removes duplicates. A total order selected later can linearize concurrent events, but adds a tie-breaking convention absent from the partial order. Further, these clock laws do not prove that every payload operation commutes: the payload must have a compatible monotone merge or separately justified operation semantics. A causal DAG and its topological order are consequently bookkeeping structures, not automatic certificates of an arithmetic computation.',
 'Take a=(2,0,1),b=(1,3,1),c=(0,2,4).',
 'a and b are incomparable because each exceeds the other in one coordinate. Both(a∨b)∨c and a∨(b∨c) equal(2,3,4); replaying b changes nothing. By contrast, writing a register to7 then9 can differ from reversing those writes unless a conflict rule is specified.',
 'The exact merge theorem identifies both the invariant state and the source of unresolved concurrency.',
 'Vector clocks alone do not establish consensus, serializability, security or availability. Clock discipline and payload semantics are essential assumptions.',
 'Define a payload lattice, prove every update inflationary and derive read semantics under an explicit message-delivery model.')
add(9,'Monte Carlo disruption models, Bayesian risk','Bayesian prediction differs from plug-in independence','Probability',
 'How do parameter uncertainty and simulation error differ in a finite reliability model?',
 'The proposal named Bayesian risk and Monte Carlo disruption models without a recovered dataset. A fully stipulated Bernoulli model yields exact posterior predictions and reveals dependence caused by a shared unknown parameter.',
 ['Let p have Beta(1,1) prior and let observations be independent Bernoulli(p) conditional on p. After s successes and f failures the posterior is Beta(s+1,f+1). No actual reliability measurements are inferred here.'],
 'Posterior moments govern joint predictions',
 'After three successes and one failure, E[p]=2/3 and E[p²]=10/21. Two future successes therefore have probability10/21, exceeding the plug-in value4/9 by2/63.',
 'The likelihood multiplies the uniform prior by p³(1−p), giving Beta(4,2). Beta-integral ratios give E[p]=4/6 and E[p²]=4·5/(6·7). Conditional independence yields P(X₁=X₂=1|p)=p²; averaging uses E[p²], not(E[p])². The difference is Var(p)=2/63 and represents parameter uncertainty. Separately, for N independent Bernoulli simulations at a fixed stipulated probability, Hoeffding’s inequality gives P(|p̂−p|≥ε)≤2exp(−2Nε²). That bound controls simulation sampling error under the model, not the model’s adequacy or posterior uncertainty. It cannot justify independence of actual failures.',
 'Compare exact prediction with a separate fixed-probability simulation target ε=0.05,δ=0.05.',
 'The posterior two-success probability is0.476190…, while plug-in independence gives0.444444…. The Hoeffding sample bound is N≥ceil(log40/(2·0.05²))=738 for the separate simulation problem.',
 'A simulation may be accurate about an assumed model while uncertainty in its parameter remains substantial.',
 'No supply-chain or financial probability is estimated. The prior, likelihood and conditional independence are hypotheses; correlated observations require another model.',
 'Compare a shared-parameter model with a specified dependence model and report posterior predictive and Monte Carlo uncertainty separately.')
add(10,'PID/LQR','A scalar LQR controller has an exact value certificate','Optimal control',
 'Which feedback minimizes a stated quadratic cost, and what proves both its stability and optimum?',
 'LQR was named in the architecture list. A normalized scalar example can be solved completely rather than supplying unexplained gains. Completion of squares also exposes the terminal condition needed for an infinite-horizon argument.',
 ['Let x′=x+u,x(0)=x₀ and J=∫₀∞(x²+u²)dt. Compare controls of finite cost with x(t)→0. Put P=1+√2, the positive root of2P−P²+1=0.'],
 'Stabilizing Riccati solution',
 'The feedback u=−Px gives x(t)=x₀e^(−√2t), achieves J=Px₀² and minimizes J in the declared admissible class.',
 'For V=Px², V′=2Px²+2Pxu. Since2P+1=P², x²+u²+V′=(u+Px)². Integration to T gives J_T=Px₀²−Px(T)²+∫₀ᵀ(u+Px)²dt. The terminal term tends to zero in the stated class, proving the lower bound. The feedback makes the square vanish and has decay rate1−P=−√2, so it attains equality. The other Riccati root1−√2 gives unstable feedback and is not a minimizing infinite-horizon certificate. Thus choosing the stabilizing solution is part of the theorem, not a numerical preference.',
 'Take x₀=1 and compare with u=−2x.',
 'The optimal cost is1+√2≈2.414214. Under u=−2x, x=e^(−t) and J=∫5e^(−2t)dt=5/2, strictly larger. The gap is the integral of the nonzero completed square.',
 'The plant, cost, admissible class, stability rate and value are all explicit and independently checkable.',
 'This scalar model assumes exact state observation and no constraints or disturbances. It gives no operational prescription or performance guarantee for an original apparatus.',
 'Add a bounded-input constraint and derive the changed value equation and invariant region before comparing policies.',[('Åström and Murray, Feedback Systems','https://fbsbook.org/')])
add(11,'Proposed ARF:','Normalized conformal growth preserves area, not every proposed entropy','Geometric evolution',
 'Which consequence follows from the stated surface-metric evolution, and which monotonicity claims need additional equations?',
 'The July10 TAG response proposed a smoothed-curvature flow and an entropy-like expression. A precise area identity follows directly from the metric equation. It does not justify independently prescribing the curvature’s heat equation or declaring the proposed functional monotone.',
 ['Let M be a closed smooth surface with time-dependent metric g. Let f=K_ε−mean_g(K_ε), where the mean is the area-weighted integral divided by total area. Assume g′=−2fg. The smoothing operator and its kernel still require their own definitions.'],
 'Area preservation under mean-zero conformal velocity',
 'The flow satisfies dA_g/dt=−2f dA_g and d Area(M,g)/dt=0. If K_ε is spatially constant, the metric is stationary.',
 'For a metric variation h, differentiating the volume density gives(d/dt)dA=(1/2)tr_g(h)dA. In dimension two, tr_g(−2fg)=−4f, hence the density derivative is−2f. Integrating yields−2∫f dA=0 by the definition of the mean. This calculation needs no special smoothing theorem. By contrast, Gaussian curvature is determined by g, and under this conformal velocity its evolution is K′=Δ_g f+2Kf. An independently stipulated equation K_ε′=Δ_gK_ε−μK_ε is therefore not automatically compatible with the metric flow, especially when the smoothing operator itself depends on time. Differentiating a proposed entropy must include all these metric and operator variations.',
 'Compare a round sphere with a spatially varying formal curvature input.',
 'On a round sphere K_ε is constant for any normalized symmetry-preserving smoothing, so f=0 and g′=0. For a varying input the local area densities may grow or shrink, but their total derivative remains zero. Area preservation alone does not imply pointwise curvature decay.',
 'The actual proved geometric invariant is total area. Other desired dissipation claims remain named problems with missing hypotheses.',
 'No existence theorem, long-time regularity or empirical growth model is established. A kernel must be normalized relative to the changing measure before its mean-preserving properties can be used.',
 'Specify the smoothing operator on evolving metrics and derive the complete first variation of the proposed functional; then prove a sign or exhibit a counterexample.')
add(12,'diffusion \\(\\partial_t','A fixed-geometry heat model has a complete energy law','Partial differential equations',
 'Under which separate fixed-metric assumptions does the proposed diffusion equation actually dissipate its quadratic energy?',
 'A fixed-domain heat equation gives a rigorous comparison for the historical entropy proposal. It separates a solvable linear evolution from a coupled geometric-flow claim. Both the zeroth-order and gradient energies can be differentiated exactly.',
 ['On a closed fixed Riemannian manifold use Δ=div grad, so ∫vΔv=−∫|∇v|². Let v_t=Δv−μv with μ≥0 and smooth real v. For λ≥0 define Θ=∫(v²+λ|∇v|²)dA.'],
 'Dissipation of a Sobolev quadratic energy',
 'Θ′=−2∫|∇v|²−2λ∫(Δv)²−2μΘ≤0. A Laplacian eigenmode with−Δφ=κφ decays as e^(−(κ+μ)t).',
 'Differentiate∫v² to obtain2∫v(Δv−μv), then integrate by parts. Differentiate the gradient term to get2λ∫∇v·∇(Δv−μv). A second integration by parts gives−2λ∫(Δv)²−2λμ∫|∇v|². Adding the two identities proves the formula. Boundary terms vanish because the manifold is closed; periodic boundaries provide the same cancellation. Substituting v=a(t)φ into the PDE gives a′=−(κ+μ)a. If μ=0, the constant mode is conserved, showing why nonincreasing energy does not imply convergence to zero without a mean-zero condition or a spectral gap.',
 'Use a2π-periodic circle, v(x,0)=cosx, μ=1 and λ=2.',
 'Here κ=1 and v=e^(−2t)cosx. Both∫cos²x and∫sin²x equalπ, so Θ=3πe^(−4t) and Θ′=−12πe^(−4t), exactly matching the three terms in the energy identity.',
 'A claimed entropy now has a declared norm, an explicit PDE and an equality proving its monotonicity.',
 'The metric and measure are fixed here. Applying this proof to an evolving curvature smoothing would omit terms and is not justified by the historical formal analogy.',
 'For a time-dependent metric, retain every volume and gradient variation and determine what extra conditions recover a dissipative functional.')
add(13,'entropy := log (1 + ext1 dimension)','The dimension of Ext is not the splitting class of one extension','Homological algebra',
 'Can log(1+dim Ext¹) detect whether a particular exact sequence splits?',
 'The July12 toy asserted entropy zero exactly when a triangle splits. That statement is generally false if entropy uses the dimension of the whole Ext group rather than the particular extension class. An elementary dual-number module gives both a split and a nonsplit sequence with identical endpoint Ext dimension.',
 ['Let R=k[ε]/(ε²) and let k=R/(ε). Equivalence classes of short exact sequences0→k→M→k→0 form Ext¹_R(k,k), whose zero class is the split extension. Group dimension counts all possible classes, not the chosen one.'],
 'An exact counterexample to a dimension-based splitting test',
 'Ext¹_R(k,k)≅k. Both the split extension with M=k⊕k and the nonsplit extension with M=R have the same endpoints. Thus log(1+dim_k Ext¹)=log2 for both, although only the first splits.',
 'A free resolution of k has every positive differential multiplication by ε: …→R→R→R→k→0. Its exactness follows because ker(ε)=εR=im(ε). Applying Hom_R(−,k) makes every differential zero, since ε acts trivially on k. Therefore Ext¹≅k. The sequence0→k→R→k→0, with injection1↦ε and quotient reduction, cannot have an R-linear section: the image v of1 would satisfy εv=0, forcing v∈εR, but then its quotient is zero. The direct-sum sequence splits by its coordinate inclusion. Their Ext dimension is identical because endpoints are identical.',
 'Compare the two middle modules R and k⊕k.',
 'In R the nilpotent ε acts nontrivially, linking the two factors; on k⊕k it acts as zero. Both have vector-space dimension two, so dimensions alone cannot distinguish the extension. The actual obstruction is the zero or nonzero class in Ext¹.',
 'A useful obstruction functional must depend on the class and a justified zero test, not just the size of its ambient group.',
 'Over ordinary vector spaces all short exact sequences split, so that category supplies an even weaker toy. This correction does not construct a derived-motivic or BSD entropy.',
 'Represent the actual extension cocycle, compute whether it is a coboundary, and only then design a class-sensitive invariant with a proved splitting criterion.')
add(14,'with coordinates \\(n,q,s,z','The arithmetic-ribbon ODE is an exact scalar connection','Differential equations',
 'What follows from the recovered scalar ODE before adding motivic, quantum or topological interpretations?',
 'The July16 ribbon equation can be integrated exactly when its variables and parameter are defined. That gives a legitimate classical connection and modulus law. It does not make integer labels differentiable or create a nonzero curvature from constant scalar coefficients.',
 ['Fix p>1. Let n,q,s,θ be differentiable real functions and let T′=T[iπn′−(logp)q′−2(logp)s′+iθ′]. If n is required to be integer-valued and continuous, it is locally constant.'],
 'Exact integration and modulus evolution',
 'T(t)=T(0)exp(iπΔn−(logp)Δq−2(logp)Δs+iΔθ). Hence |T(t)|/|T(0)|=p^(−Δq−2Δs). The corresponding scalar connection one-form is closed for fixed p.',
 'Define F=iπn−(logp)q−2(logp)s+iθ. Differentiation shows(T e^(−F))′=0, proving the formula even when T(0)=0. Taking real parts of F proves the modulus identity. Locally the connection is dF, so its curvature d(dF) is zero. If θ is an angular coordinate, dθ is locally exact but its loop integral can be2πm; exponentiation still gives unit holonomy e^(2πim)=1 in this normalization. Allowing p to vary adds−(q+2s)dp/p to dF, so the fixed-p formula cannot be reused unchanged.',
 'Take p=2,n=q=0,s=t and θ=3t.',
 'The solution is T(t)=T(0)2^(−2t)e^(3it), so the modulus at t=1 is one quarter of its initial value. A separate target|z|=2^(−s) would require a different variable definition or coefficient; it is not the same modulus law automatically.',
 'The equation produces an explicit amplitude and phase law, with every additional interpretation remaining separate.',
 'No perfectoid tilting, Adams differential, motivic trace or physical measurement theorem follows from scalar integration. Derivatives of discrete labels require a specified interpolation or a difference equation.',
 'Specify the base space, line bundle and transition functions, then compute genuine global holonomy and compare a chosen discrete version with the continuous solution.')
add(15,'commutators \\([\\hat s','Finite matrices cannot satisfy an exact canonical commutator','Operator algebra',
 'Can the proposed ribbon operators satisfy[s,θ]=iℏI in a finite prototype, and what finite relation is actually possible?',
 'The assistant promoted formal commutators into legitimate structures without an operator domain. A trace argument gives an immediate obstruction for finite matrices. Clock and shift matrices provide a precise alternative, with an explicit deformation phase rather than a false finite canonical pair.',
 ['For N×N matrices define[A,B]=AB−BA. Fix ℏ≠0. On C^N let ω=e^(2πi/N), Xe_j=e_{j+1 mod N} and Ze_j=ω^je_j.'],
 'Trace obstruction and the finite Weyl pair',
 'No finite matrices satisfy[A,B]=iℏI. The clock and shift instead satisfy ZX=ωXZ, X^N=Z^N=I, and are unitary.',
 'Cyclicity gives tr(AB−BA)=0, while tr(iℏI)=iℏN≠0, a contradiction. For the finite pair, ZXe_j=ω^(j+1)e_{j+1} and XZe_j=ω^je_{j+1}, proving the Weyl relation on a basis. Each map permutes or phases an orthonormal basis, so it is unitary. Iterating N times returns every basis vector. This multiplicative relation does not contradict the trace obstruction and should not be silently differentiated into an exact additive commutator of finite logarithms. Infinite-dimensional canonical operators require dense invariant domains and careful treatment of unbounded products.',
 'Take N=3.',
 'The clock is diag(1,ω,ω²), and the shift is the cyclic permutation matrix. Their order-dependent product differs by the cube root of unity ω. Any claimed three-state realization of[A,B]=iℏI fails before its eigenvalues are computed.',
 'A prototype must choose a finite Weyl model, an approximation with quantified error, or a properly defined infinite-dimensional representation.',
 'This does not rule out canonical commutation relations on suitable infinite-dimensional spaces. It rules out the exact finite identity, not all discretizations or physical models.',
 'Choose an explicit discretization and bound its commutator error on a stated low-energy subspace instead of claiming an exact finite canonical relation.')
add(16,'24/240, Ramanujan','The numbers 24 and 240 arise from different exact constructions','Lattices and modular forms',
 'Which count produces240, which q-product produces24, and what theorem is needed to connect either with moonshine?',
 'The July17 discussion connected E₈, Ramanujan coefficients, the Leech lattice and tmf. An exact root count and a formal product calculation retain real mathematics without treating repeated integers as evidence of an unspecified common mechanism.',
 ['The E₈ roots consist of vectors with two coordinates±1 and six zeros, together with half-integral vectors(±1/2,…,±1/2) having an even number of minus signs. Every root has squared length2. Define Δ(q)=q∏_{m≥1}(1−q^m)^24.'],
 'Root count and the first discriminant coefficients',
 'The displayed E₈ root set has112+128=240 elements. The formal product gives Δ=q−24q²+252q³−1472q⁴+…. These are exact statements about different defined objects.',
 'Choose the two nonzero integral positions in C(8,2)=28 ways and their signs in four ways, giving112 roots. Among the2⁸ half-integral sign choices exactly half have even parity, giving128. For Δ, each coefficient depends on finitely many factors. Through q³ in the product, use(1−q)^24,(1−q²)^24,(1−q³)^24; the binomial expansion gives1−24q+252q²−1472q³. The deeper classical identity θ_E8=E₄ follows from theta modularity for the even unimodular rank-eight lattice and the one-dimensional weight-four modular-form space. It is a separate theorem; the root count checks its first nonconstant coefficient240 rather than proving every possible moonshine correspondence.',
 'Compare the initial E₄ and Δ expansions.',
 'E₄=1+240Σ_{n≥1}σ₃(n)q^n starts1+240q+2160q²+6720q³. Δ starts q−24q²+252q³. A coefficient24 in one expression is not the E₈ root count240, and neither equals the Leech kissing number196560.',
 'The constants acquire meaning through explicit counting and transformation laws, not arithmetic resemblance alone.',
 'No VOA, tmf orientation or sporadic-group action is constructed. Those claims need actual representations, graded spaces and identities, not a list of familiar constants.',
 'Construct the proposed graded representation and verify enough exact character data together with a uniqueness theorem, rather than inferring a module from a few dimensions.',[LIE])
add(17,'periods/multiple zeta values','A multiple-zeta product has an exact combinatorial decomposition','Periods',
 'What concrete identity illustrates period multiplication before invoking a motivic Galois symmetry?',
 'The historical synthesis mentioned multiple zeta values and motivic symmetry. A convergent double-sum identity is a rigorous local piece of that programme. It demonstrates an algebraic relation while keeping the motivic lift and its symmetry group as additional constructions.',
 ['For a,b>1 define ζ(a)=Σ_{m≥1}m^(−a) and ζ(a,b)=Σ_{m>n≥1}m^(−a)n^(−b). Finite truncations use the same upper bound N on both indices. These positive series converge absolutely.'],
 'A stuffle identity',
 'ζ(2)ζ(3)=ζ(2,3)+ζ(3,2)+ζ(5). The identical decomposition holds for finite harmonic sums when all summation ranges are truncated consistently.',
 'Expand the product as a sum over all ordered pairs(m,n). Partition the index set into m>n,n>m and m=n. On the first part the summand is m^(−2)n^(−3), giving ζ(2,3). Interchanging names on the second yields ζ(3,2). The diagonal gives Σm^(−5). Absolute convergence justifies partitioning and reindexing; alternatively perform the identity for finite N and use monotone convergence. This proof is an exact relation among analytic numbers. Calling them motivic periods requires a specified category and comparison map; the summation identity by itself does not construct the motivic Galois group or prove algebraic independence.',
 'Truncate at N=2.',
 'H₂^(2)=5/4 and H₂^(3)=9/8, whose product is45/32. The m>n contributions are1/4 and1/8, while H₂^(5)=33/32. Their sum is(8+4+33)/32=45/32 exactly.',
 'The example provides a replayable finite certificate and a convergent limiting proof, with no numerical-recognition step.',
 'One product identity does not classify all multiple-zeta relations, determine transcendence degrees or establish a geometric Langlands correspondence.',
 'Define the relevant iterated integrals, derive their shuffle relations as well, and compare the two algebra structures before proposing a motivic interpretation.')
add(18,'Connes spectral triples','A two-point spectral triple computes a genuine distance','Noncommutative geometry',
 'Which algebra, Hilbert space and operator define the proposed spectral geometry, and what distance do they produce?',
 'The source named spectral triples and physics links without defining an operator. A complete two-point triple shows how an operator norm can encode a metric. It is a classical finite example, not a construction of the Standard Model or an arithmetic spectral realization.',
 ['Represent A=C² on H=C² by diag(a,b). Let D=[[0,m],[overline m,0]] with m≠0. For the two evaluation states δ₁,δ₂, define distance sup{|a−b|:a,b real,||[D,diag(a,b)]||≤1}.'],
 'Exact two-point distance',
 'The commutator norm equals|m||a−b|, so the spectral distance is1/|m|. D is self-adjoint and has compact resolvent because H is finite-dimensional.',
 'Matrix multiplication gives[D,diag(a,b)]=[[0,m(b−a)],[overline m(a−b),0]]. Its adjoint-times-itself is|m|²|a−b|²I, so both singular values equal|m||a−b|. The constraint is therefore|a−b|≤1/|m|, and equality is attained by a=1/|m|,b=0. The supremum is thus exact, not merely bounded. Scaling D by a positive factor c divides the distance by c. If m=0 the constraint imposes no restriction on a−b and the distance is infinite, illustrating why operator coupling is essential. More elaborate triples need domains, representation and regularity conditions that are trivial only in this finite case.',
 'Take m=2.',
 'The allowed difference is at most1/2, and a=1/2,b=0 attains it. The eigenvalues of D are±2, while the distance is1/2; these are related by the chosen operator, not by a free numerical identification.',
 'Every component of the metric construction is now supplied and its consequence proved.',
 'No field theory, arithmetic L-function or higher-dimensional geometry follows from this two-point example. A named spectral triple without an algebra action and analytic domain is incomplete.',
 'For a finite weighted graph triple, derive its distance constraints and determine exactly when the result agrees with a chosen path metric.')
add(19,'Kashiwara crystals/quantum groups','An sl₂ crystal records weights without inventing a quantum model','Representation theory',
 'Which combinatorial operators and weight multiplicities make a crystal claim precise?',
 'The July17 synthesis named crystal bases and quantum groups. The rank-one crystal supplies an explicit, checkable combinatorial structure and character identity. It does not turn a crystal diagram into a physical lattice or an arbitrary categorical symmetry.',
 ['For m≥0, B(m)={0,…,m} has weight wt(k)=m−2k. Define lowering f(k)=k+1 when k<m and raising e(k)=k−1 when k>0, with an undefined symbol outside the chain. Its formal character is χ_m(z)=Σ_{k=0}^mz^(m−2k).'],
 'A rank-one character decomposition',
 'For m≥1, χ_m(z)(z+z⁻¹)=χ_{m+1}(z)+χ_{m−1}(z). Raising and lowering change weights by±2 and are inverse wherever both are defined.',
 'The operator assertions follow directly from the chain definitions. For the character identity multiply the finite geometric-weight sum by z and z⁻¹. The extreme weights m+1 and−m−1 occur once; every interior weight from m−1 to−m+1 occurs twice. χ_{m+1} supplies one copy of every weight in that range, and χ_{m−1} supplies the second interior copy. This proves the identity as a Laurent polynomial. It matches the classical sl₂ tensor decomposition V_m⊗V_1≅V_{m+1}⊕V_{m−1}, but constructing a full quantum-group crystal basis entails a module, integral form and limiting procedure beyond this combinatorial verification.',
 'Use m=2.',
 'B(2) has weights2,0,−2. Multiplication byχ₁=z+z⁻¹ gives z³+2z+2z⁻¹+z⁻³, equal toχ₃+χ₁. Evaluating at z=1 gives3·2=4+2, the corresponding dimension check.',
 'The diagram and coefficients are now defined objects with exact identities, rather than symbols standing in for unconstructed symmetry.',
 'The rank-one example does not produce an E₈ crystal, a canonical basis for every representation or a link to the proposed ribbon operators.',
 'Specify the Cartan data and tensor-product rule for a higher-rank module, then verify its crystal axioms and character against an independent representation computation.',[LIE])
add(20,'f=x^2+2y^2+3z^2','A genuine 7-adic obstruction explains the ternary exclusions','Quadratic forms',
 'Which claimed nonrepresentations follow from an exact congruence theorem for the recovered ternary form?',
 'The July23 form has a real arithmetic obstruction. It can be proved by completing squares modulo seven and descending through powers of49, without any speculative tmf or Langlands construction. This is stronger than checking a few missing values numerically, but it remains a necessary condition rather than a complete representation classification.',
 ['Let F(x,y,z)=x²+2y²+3z²+xz+yz on Z³. For N≠0 write v₇(N) for its7-adic valuation. The nonzero quadratic residues modulo seven are1,2,4.'],
 'A local nonrepresentation family',
 'If N=7^(2k+1)u with7∤u and u a quadratic residue modulo seven, then F never represents N. In particular7,14,28,56,63,77 are excluded.',
 'Modulo seven, F=(x+4z)²+2(y+2z)². Because−2≡5 is not a square modulo seven, F≡0 forces x≡3z and y≡5z. Write x=3z+7a,y=5z+7b. Expansion gives F/7=10z²+7az+21bz+7a²+14b², hence F/7≡3z². If v₇(F)=1, its unit part must lie in{3,5,6}, the nonresidues, not{1,2,4}. If49 divides F, this congruence forces z≡0 and then x,y≡0; dividing all coordinates by seven replaces F by F/49. Iterating reduces any odd valuation to the first case, proving the theorem. Positivity and a finite search bound follow independently from8F=2(2x+z)²+(4y+z)²+21z².',
 'Apply the criterion to the six historical numbers.',
 'Their quotients by seven are1,2,4,8,9,11, reducing to1,2,4,1,2,4. Every valuation is one and every unit residue is excluded. Conversely the theorem does not claim every allowed local residue is globally represented.',
 'The historical list has an exact local explanation, with a proved infinite family rather than an inferred conceptual analogy.',
 'Local admissibility need not imply integral representation. Genus, spinor genus and possible exceptional square classes require additional work.',
 'Compute all local conditions and investigate whether they are sufficient for this specific form, using a proved genus/spinor-genus analysis rather than finite extrapolation.',[ANT])
add(21,'cyclic number','Cyclic decimals and the tuple identities require separate proofs','Elementary number theory',
 'Which exact identities hold for142857 and the tuple(384,840,1680,1840), and what inference do they permit?',
 'The assistant corrected two false rational equalities in the July23 exchange. The tuple’s modular pattern and the repeating decimal are real, but they do not restore those equalities or prove a bridge to an obstruction theory. Exact fractions give a complete audit.',
 ['The decimal period of1/7 is the multiplicative order of10 modulo7. Let a=384,b=840,c=1680,d=1840. Arithmetic below is over integers and rational numbers, with no inferred physical interpretation.'],
 'Exact period, residues and corrected fractions',
 '142857=(10⁶−1)/7, ord₇(10)=6, and(a,b,c,d)≡(−1,0,0,−1)mod7. But a/b+c/d=1103/805, and a(d−c)/b=512/7.',
 'The residues10^j mod7 for j=0,…,6 are1,3,2,6,4,5,1, so the first return occurs at six. Long division then gives1/7=0.overline142857. Multiplying a six-digit repeating block by10 rotates it modulo10⁶−1, explaining the cyclic pattern without a new geometric object. For the tuple reduce each integer modulo seven directly. Rational simplification gives384/840=16/35 and1680/1840=21/23; their sum is(368+735)/805=1103/805. Also384·160/840=512/7. These exact values differ from3/2 and2048 respectively; modular agreement of selected integers cannot force either rational identity.',
 'Check the first cyclic multiple and the historical target comparison.',
 '2·142857=285714, a rotation. Meanwhile1103/805≈1.3701863354, and512/7≈73.14285714. The common appearance of a seventh does not identify these quantities with a BPS invariant or a categorical boundary.',
 'Every retained arithmetic observation is justified individually, while the proposed structural analogy remains unproved.',
 'A repeating decimal and congruence tuple do not define a genus-three curve, an entropy, a motivic lift or a tmf class.',
 'If a geometric interpretation is intended, define the objects and the map producing these exact integers, then prove its compatibility with the displayed arithmetic rather than fitting an analogy afterward.')
add(22,'supplied guided July computation','The finite ray class group of Q(√10) can be computed exactly','Class field theory',
 'What does the stated finite modulus actually mean, and which ray group follows without inventing infinite-place data?',
 'The July25 arithmetic contains enough exact data for a real computation. Treat the modulus as the stated finite ideal, separately from later contradictory narrow-modulus assertions. This gives a class-group proof and an explicit ray-kernel calculation.',
 ['Let K=Q(√10), O=Z[√10], discriminant40, P₂=(2,√10), and m=(3,1+√10). Its quotient is F₃ through √10↦−1, so Norm(m)=3. The finite ray group uses principal generators congruent to1 modulo m, without a positivity condition.'],
 'Finite ray group and ordinary class group',
 'Cl(O)≅C₂ and Cl_m(O)≅Cl(O). Also narrow and ordinary class groups agree for modulus1 because the units realize all real sign patterns.',
 'Minkowski gives a representative of every class with norm≤√40/2<3.163. Thus norm-two and norm-three primes generate. P₂²=(2), while P₂ is nonprincipal: an element of norm±2 would require a²−10b²=±2, impossible modulo five. The element2+√10 has norm−6 and factors as P₂ times the norm-three prime with √10≡1. The conjugate norm-three prime has inverse class; hence both have the same order-two class as P₂. These exhaust the Minkowski generators, proving class number two. The ray exact sequence O×→(O/m)×→Cl_m→Cl→1 has trivial kernel term because−1 maps to the nontrivial unit of F₃. Finally3+√10 has norm−1 and signs(+,-); together with−1 it realizes all four real sign patterns, proving narrow=ordinary.',
 'Audit the modulus norm and the residue-unit image.',
 'The map a+b√10↦a−b modulo three has kernel m. The two residue units are1,2, both images of global units1,−1. No extra ray factor survives. A computation treating m as norm9 would use the wrong quotient ring.',
 'The ray answer is now tied to a precise modulus and exact sequence rather than a guessed class field.',
 'Adding real places to this finite modulus changes the ray condition and requires recomputing the joint residue/sign image; the separate narrow-modulus1 result does not settle every combined modulus.',
 'Write the full finite and infinite modulus, compute its unit image, and construct its Artin map before claiming principalization or an inversion algorithm.',[ANT,CFT])
add(23,'ray field Q(√10,i)','The claimed Hilbert class field has the wrong ramification','Class field theory',
 'Which quadratic extension of Q(√10) is unramified, and does the proposed polynomial generate it?',
 'The assistant identified K(i) as a Hilbert class field and later gave incompatible narrow data. Discriminants and real embeddings give decisive exact tests. The resulting correction is an actual class-field identification, using the independently proved class number two.',
 ['For a biquadratic extension of Q, its field discriminant is the product of the discriminants of its three quadratic subfields. For L/K, Disc(L/Q)=Disc(K/Q)^[L:K]·Norm_K/Q(disc(L/K)). Use the ordinary Hilbert class field, unramified also at real places.'],
 'The unramified quadratic extension',
 'The Hilbert class field of K=Q(√10) is L=Q(√2,√5). The proposed K(i) is ramified and is not that field. The polynomial x⁴−18x²+121 is instead satisfied by√10+i.',
 'The quadratic discriminants for L are8,5,40, whose product is1600=40². Hence the relative discriminant has norm one and is the unit ideal, so no finite prime ramifies. L is totally real, so no real place ramifies either. It is a nontrivial degree-two extension of K. Since Cl(K) has order two, class field theory identifies it with the Hilbert class field. For K(i), the subfield discriminants are40,−4,−40, whose product is6400. Its relative discriminant norm is6400/40²=4, already excluding finite unramifiedness; its complex embeddings also ramify the real places. Finally(√10+i)²=9+2i√10, so squaring the shifted square gives the stated quartic.',
 'Compare two primitive-element polynomials.',
 '√2+√5 satisfies x⁴−14x²+9=0, whereas√10+i satisfies x⁴−18x²+121=0. A polynomial defining a degree-four extension does not identify its ramification or make it the desired class field. Polynomial discriminants may also differ from field discriminants by an index square.',
 'The correction distinguishes a genuine unramified extension from a plausible-looking but wrong defining polynomial.',
 'The biquadratic discriminant theorem and global class-field correspondence are invoked as classical results. This does not compute arbitrary ray fields or Stark units.',
 'Construct integral bases and exact relative discriminant ideals for any further proposed field, then verify its Artin behavior at several unramified primes.',[ANT,CFT])
add(24,'assistant-generated D₄ example','A real quartic is not its D₄ splitting field','Galois theory',
 'What are the actual degree, normal closure and rational ray-group size in the recovered D₄ example?',
 'A later July25 source explicitly describes an assistant-generated example, despite being nested in a user-fact retrieval line. It is therefore treated as assistant evidence. Exact field containment and elementary congruence groups refute its central identifications.',
 ['Let α=2^(1/4)>0. Since α²=√2, Q(α,√2)=Q(α). The proposed rational modulus is136∞, where the infinite place imposes positive principal generators.'],
 'Normal closure and ray-group audit',
 'Q(α) has degree four and is not normal. The splitting field of x⁴−2 is Q(α,i), of degree eight with dihedral group D₄. The ray class group of Q for136∞ is(Z/136Z)×, of order64, not four.',
 'Eisenstein at two proves x⁴−2 irreducible. Q(α) is real and therefore omits the root iα, so it is not a splitting field. Adjoining i doubles the degree. Automorphisms r:α↦iα,i↦i and s:α↦α,i↦−i obey r⁴=s²=1 and srs=r⁻¹, giving the eight-element dihedral group. For the ray group, Q has trivial ordinary class group, and positive principal generators leave the full residue-unit group modulo136=8·17. CRT gives orderφ(8)φ(17)=4·16=64. Without the infinite place the quotient by±1 would instead have order32. Neither convention gives C₂².',
 'Check the asserted split-prime17 behavior.',
 'Modulo17, x⁴−2=(x²−6)(x²+6). Neither6 nor11 is a square modulo17, so both quadratics are irreducible and17 does not split into four degree-one primes in Q(α). The exact identityα⁴=2 also makes(α)^4=(2), but the prime ideal(α) is already principal.',
 'Degree, normality, modulus and splitting are independent checks, all of which must precede an arithmetic interpretation.',
 'No recovered numerical L-derivative or Stark-unit claim is validated. A nonabelian D₄ extension cannot itself be identified with an abelian ray class field over Q.',
 'Compute explicit Frobenius cycle types and the abelian subextensions of the actual normal closure, with each modulus stated separately.',[ANT,CFT],evidence='A')
add(25,'g(n)=8n^2-5n+3','The quadratic sequence has a rational generating function','Generating functions',
 'What exact recurrence and q-series follow from the recovered g(n), and what modular claim remains unproved?',
 'The July27 sequence is a well-defined elementary object. Its recurrence, positivity and generating function can be proved completely. Those statements do not establish mock modularity or a sporadic-group representation.',
 ['For n≥0 let g(n)=8n²−5n+3 and a_n=240+24g(n). Let F(q)=Σ_{n≥0}a_nq^n, formally or analytically for|q|<1.'],
 'Exact rational series',
 'g(0)=3, g(n)−g(n−1)=16n−13, and F(q)=(312−552q+624q²)/(1−q)³. Also g(x)=8(x−5/16)²+71/32 for real x.',
 'Subtract the two quadratic values to obtain the first difference. Completing the square proves positivity and explains the discriminant−71 without constructing a quadratic field or moonshine object. Since a_n=312−120n+192n², substitute the formal identitiesΣq^n=(1−q)⁻¹, Σnq^n=q/(1−q)² andΣn²q^n=q(1+q)/(1−q)³. Collecting numerators yields312−552q+624q². Multiplying the denominator back gives an independent third-difference recurrence. All of this is classical polynomial enumeration. A rational q-expression is not a modular transformation law, and a negative discriminant in a quadratic polynomial does not define the missing lift or representation.',
 'Compute the initial coefficients.',
 'g(0),…,g(4) are3,6,25,60,111, so a_n begins312,384,840,1680,2904. In particular the historical tuple’s last value1840 is not a₄. The pole at q=1 has order three and matches quadratic coefficient growth.',
 'The actual sequence and its exact analytic/formal structure are now certified, while the claimed mock completion remains a separate problem.',
 'No CHL, Donaldson–Thomas, M₂₄ or E₈ module is constructed. Finitely many coefficient coincidences cannot prove such an identification.',
 'State a group, multiplier, weight and transformation law; determine whether a correctly typed completion can satisfy them with this holomorphic part.')
add(26,'xi_{3/2}','The proposed mock-modular shadow has incompatible weight and domain','Harmonic Maass forms',
 'Does the asserted shadow identity have the correct weight and a convergent theta argument?',
 'The prior assistant claimed a weight3/2 completion with E₈ theta shadow and uniqueness. Before any coefficient fitting, the operator’s transformation weight and the analytic domain must agree. Two elementary checks already expose the missing structure.',
 ['For τ=x+iy in the upper half-plane define ξ_kF=2iy^k overline(∂F/∂overlineτ). On appropriately transforming harmonic Maass forms it has weight2−k. A positive-definite rank-eight theta series has weight four and converges for positive imaginary argument.'],
 'A type obstruction, with a local differential example',
 'At k=3/2 the shadow weight is1/2, not four. Moreover θ_E8(−4τ) is not defined by its positive-definite theta series for y>0, because its terms grow rather than decay.',
 'The weight2−k follows by applying the antiholomorphic chain rule to the weight-k transformation and conjugating; this is the standard ξ-operator covariance. Independently, a theta term exp(πi(−4τ)||v||²) has modulus exp(4πy||v||²), so the terms do not even tend to zero along lattice vectors. Thus the literal historical right-hand side is not a holomorphic theta function on the chosen domain. To show the differential operator concretely, take F(y)=y^(1−k). Then ∂overlineτF=(i/2)(1−k)y^(−k), so ξ_kF=1−k. The weight-k Laplacian also annihilates this local function, but that does not make it a global modular form.',
 'Set k=3/2 in the local example.',
 'F=y^(−1/2) gives ξ_{3/2}F=−1/2. This checks the derivative normalization and sign. It supplies no transformation law or cusp condition and therefore cannot repair the claimed global completion.',
 'A shadow identity needs compatible weight, multiplier, representation, domain and growth conditions before uniqueness is meaningful.',
 'These checks reject the literal assertion, not every possible vector-valued mock-Jacobi construction. A corrected theory may involve different theta kernels and operators.',
 'Choose a valid weight1/2 shadow and specify principal parts and growth conditions, then formulate a genuine existence/ambiguity theorem for the completion.',[('Bruinier and Funke, On Two Geometric Theta Lifts','https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0212286')])
add(27,'A₂ formulas preserved','A₂ and D₄ lattice data precede any Jacobi completion','Root lattices',
 'Which determinants and root counts follow from the actual Gram matrices, and which claimed modular structures need more?',
 'The July27 lattice formulas retain useful exact mathematics. Gram matrices define quadratic forms, covolumes and theta coefficients, while a mock-Jacobi completion additionally requires elliptic and modular transformation laws not supplied by the matrix alone.',
 ['For an even positive lattice with Gram matrix S, set Q(v)=vᵀSv/2. The A₂ matrix is[[2,1],[1,2]]. The D₄ source matrix has diagonal2 and off-diagonal−1 along edges12,23,24.'],
 'Determinants and initial theta coefficients',
 'The A₂ form x²+xy+y² has determinant3 and six vectors of Q=1. The D₄ Gram determinant is4 and it has24 roots of Q=1. Their theta series begin1+6q+… and1+24q+… respectively.',
 'For A₂,4Q=(2x+y)²+3y², proving positivity and bounding all Q=1 solutions. They are(±1,0),(0,±1),(1,−1),(−1,1). Its determinant is4−1=3. For D₄, reorder the central node last: the Schur complement of the three diagonal2 leaves is2−3/2=1/2, so the determinant is2³·1/2=4 and positivity follows. Its standard isometric realization is{v∈Z⁴:Σv_i even}, with roots±e_i±e_j. Choosing the pair of positions and four signs gives6·4=24. Theta coefficients count vectors of a fixed norm by definition.',
 'Compare lattice rank with ordinary theta weight.',
 'The classical theta transformation has weight rank/2, giving weight one for A₂ and weight two for D₄, with the appropriate level/representation. A claimed weight-two Jacobi object with an A₂-related shadow needs an explicitly different construction; the Gram matrix itself does not prove it.',
 'The exact lattice data survive independently of unproved VOA and moonshine assignments.',
 'No completion, Eichler integral, elliptic variable or multiplier was recovered in a verifiable form. Determinant and root count do not classify every lattice or its automorphism action.',
 'Construct the discriminant group and vector-valued theta representation, then formulate the precise Jacobi or mock transformation problem.',[LIE])
add(28,'Exponential Density Law','A fitted density line and three beta values do not define a theory','Statistical identifiability',
 'Which quantities are actually defined, and do the supplied numbers support the proposed exponential law?',
 'The July28 framework assigned densities to Lie/sporadic pairs, cohomology degrees and a beta function. The numerical and logical claims can be audited without endorsing a new geometry. Definitions and independent predictions must precede a fitted law.',
 ['Use the stated ranks4,6,7,8 and densities9240,1320,80960,1020096. Let y_i=log₁₀density_i and proposed prediction0.514r_i+1.291. The symbol Λ_g and any density denominator still require definitions.'],
 'A direct residual test and a nonuniqueness theorem',
 'The supplied line does not fit the first two densities closely: their residuals are about0.619 and−1.254 logarithmic units. Three beta values determine no unique function, even within smooth functions.',
 'Evaluate the line at each rank and subtract from the logarithm of the stated density. A residual of−1.254 means the proposed value is too large by a factor about10^1.254, so it cannot be hidden by decimal rounding. For the beta claim, let P be the quadratic interpolant through its three stipulated inputs1/2,1,2. Every P(x)+c(x−1/2)(x−1)(x−2), c∈R, agrees at all three points and generally differs elsewhere. Thus finitely many values do not identify a generating mechanism. Likewise2+3+4+5=14 adds degree labels; it is not a computation of dimH²+dimH³+dimH⁴+dimH⁵, whose summands are Betti numbers that must be obtained from actual spaces.',
 'Use the supplied beta samples1.32132546,0.13591270,0.00178552.',
 'They can be interpolated exactly as rational decimals, but adding the cubic vanishing factor preserves every sample. The count14 remains a correct integer sum without proving a fourteen-dimensional variety or cohomology decomposition.',
 'The audit separates exact arithmetic, parameter fitting and mathematical construction.',
 'No statistical uncertainty, independently defined Λ_g, held-out predictions or geometric beta construction was recovered. These numbers cannot establish emergent integers or sporadic symmetry.',
 'Define each observable before collecting values, state a finite model class and test independent predictions with declared error and null models.')
add(29,'x^4-4x^3+1','A quartic splitting field cannot have Mathieu Galois group','Galois theory',
 'What Galois groups are even possible for the two recovered quartic polynomials?',
 'The July28 framework associated quartics with M₁₂ and M₂₄. A degree bound already rules out a literal identification of their splitting-field Galois groups. The quartics themselves are legitimate algebraic objects whose irreducibility can be checked exactly.',
 ['Let f=x⁴−4x³+1 and h=x⁴−6x³+4x²+2x+1. A characteristic-zero splitting-field group acts faithfully by permutations of the four roots, hence embeds in S₄ and has order at most24.'],
 'Irreducible quartics with small possible Galois groups',
 'Both f and h are irreducible over Q, but neither can have splitting-field group M₁₂ or M₂₄. A separate construction would be needed to relate their roots to a Mathieu representation.',
 'A rational root of either monic polynomial with constant1 must be±1, and direct substitution excludes both. A remaining factorization would be two monic integral quadratics by Gauss’s lemma. Their constant terms must both be e=1 or both e=−1. Write(x²+ax+e)(x²+bx+e). Its cubic coefficient is a+b and linear coefficient e(a+b). For f these would have to be−4 and0, impossible for e=±1. For h they would have to be−6 and2, likewise impossible. Hence both are irreducible. Independently, any automorphism fixing all four roots fixes the splitting field they generate, proving the faithful S₄ embedding. The Mathieu groups have orders greater than24, excluding them without numerical root fitting.',
 'Check the constant/cubic/linear factor constraints.',
 'For f, e(a+b)=±4 can never be0. For h it is±6 and never2. This proof does not determine whether the actual transitive subgroup is S₄,A₄,D₄,C₄ or V₄; discriminant and resolvent tests are still needed.',
 'A degree-four formula may participate in a larger representation, but its literal root-permutation group obeys an unavoidable degree bound.',
 'No Mathieu action or field discriminant was recovered. Polynomial discriminants, splitting fields and unrelated Pell fields must remain distinct.',
 'Compute exact discriminants and cubic resolvents, then identify each actual transitive subgroup and state any additional representation map explicitly.',[ANT])
add(30,'defined HCH-k','A SAT avoidance complex does not make all 2-SAT instances contractible','Combinatorial topology',
 'What does the literal avoidance complex encode, and is the claimed universal2-SAT topology correct?',
 'The early July project proposed a homological complexity hierarchy. Its simplicial construction can be made coherent, but its asserted topological classification needs independent proof. Even the empty2-CNF formula provides a decisive counterexample to universal contractibility.',
 ['For n Boolean variables use vertices x_i,¬x_i. A face contains no opposite pair and no set consisting of all negated literals of a clause. A full face chooses exactly one literal per variable; it represents the corresponding truth assignment. Reduced homology uses an explicitly chosen coefficient field.'],
 'Satisfying facets and a counterexample',
 'Full(n−1)-dimensional faces correspond exactly to satisfying assignments. With no clauses, the complex is the join of n two-point sets, the boundary of the n-dimensional crosspolytope, homeomorphic to S^(n−1). It is not contractible for n≥1.',
 'A full face satisfies a clause precisely when it does not choose all the clause’s negations. Applying that test to every clause proves the assignment correspondence. With no clauses, only opposite-pair exclusions remain. Each variable contributes a choice of either endpoint of a0-sphere, and joining these choices gives the crosspolytope boundary. Its reduced homology in degree n−1 is one-dimensional. The formula with no clauses is a valid2-CNF formula and is trivially satisfiable, yet has nontrivial topology. Therefore neither satisfiability nor ease of decision implies contractibility for this construction. An entropy or Betti profile may still be studied as an observable, but cannot inherit a complexity theorem from its name.',
 'Use two variables and no clauses.',
 'The vertices x,¬x,y,¬y form a four-cycle with edges joining choices from different variables. It has four vertices,four edges and no triangles, so β₁=1. Adding suitable clauses can delete faces and change the topology without establishing a monotone decision-complexity scale.',
 'The construction survives, while the universal2-SAT/HCH classification fails for an explicit simplest input.',
 'This does not settle any relation between topology and worst-case complexity. A filtration and an allowed reduction class must be defined before persistence or hierarchy claims are meaningful.',
 'Fix a functorial construction and test which reductions actually preserve or reflect the chosen homology; state resulting implications as theorems or explicit conjectures.')
add(31,'3-SAT→CLIQUE simplicial map','A decision reduction does not automatically inject homology','Complexity and topology',
 'Which part of the3-SAT-to-CLIQUE construction is proved, and what extra condition would make its homology map injective?',
 'The July2 response conjectured homology injectivity from a standard decision reduction. Correctness of a reduction concerns the existence of solutions, whereas injectivity concerns every cycle and boundary. These are different statements, and a small simplicial map shows the gap.',
 ['For a k-clause formula, create one graph vertex per literal occurrence. Join vertices from different clauses when their literals are not contradictory. A simplicial map sends vertices to vertices and every face to a face; repeated images may lower dimension.'],
 'The standard clique equivalence and the missing topological theorem',
 'The formula is satisfiable exactly when the occurrence graph has a k-clique. A simplicial map in general need not induce an injective homology map, even when it preserves an existence predicate.',
 'A satisfying assignment chooses a true literal in each clause. The chosen occurrences are pairwise noncontradictory and lie in different clauses, hence form a k-clique. Conversely a k-clique contains one occurrence from each clause, since no same-clause vertices are adjacent. Its literals are consistent and extend to a full satisfying assignment. Nothing in this argument constructs a chain-level retraction or proves a nonzero cycle survives. For a counterexample to automatic injectivity, map every vertex of a simplicial circle to one vertex. This is a valid simplicial map and induces the zero map H₁(S¹)→H₁(point)=0. An injective vertex map also would not suffice if the target fills a cycle with new two-simplices.',
 'Map the boundary of a triangle into the filled triangle by inclusion.',
 'The source has H₁≅F and the target H₁=0, so even an inclusion of simplicial complexes can kill homology. A genuine homology embedding needs a separate argument such as a chain retraction or a suitable relative-homology criterion.',
 'The decision reduction remains valid; the claimed obstruction hierarchy requires substantially more than that equivalence.',
 'NoP-versus-NP separation or no-reduction theorem follows. Historical PHP Betti numbers are not adopted without their actual complex and boundary matrices.',
 'Build the proposed chain maps explicitly and either construct a left inverse up to homotopy or exhibit a cycle that is killed.')
add(32,'Ollivier–Ricci curvature','Ollivier curvature is a transport calculation, not a hardness oracle','Discrete geometry',
 'Which graph measure defines the curvature, and can a computed value justify a complexity or cryptographic claim?',
 'The July3–4 frameworks used curvature, spectral gaps and Betti numbers as proposed difficulty indicators. Those observables require precise constructions. A complete elementary transport calculation shows how curvature depends on the chosen neighborhood measure, while no security or hardness theorem is inferred.',
 ['For adjacent vertices x,y at graph distance one, choose probability measures m_x,m_y and define κ(x,y)=1−W₁(m_x,m_y). W₁ minimizesΣd(u,v)π(u,v) over couplings with the declared marginals. Here use the uniform measure on neighbors, with no idleness.'],
 'Exact curvatures of two small graphs',
 'For K₂ the unique edge has κ=0. For K₃ every edge has κ=1/2 under this measure convention.',
 'In K₂, the neighbor measures are point masses at opposite endpoints, so transporting unit mass costs one. In K₃ with vertices x,y,z, the measures are(δ_y+δ_z)/2 and(δ_x+δ_z)/2. Match the common z-mass to itself and move the remaining half from y to x, costing1/2. This is optimal because the target has half a unit at x and the source has none there; all possible source points are distance one from x. Hence W₁=1/2. A different idleness convention changes the measures and can change κ, so comparison requires locked definitions. Similarly complex cochain coefficients do not by themselves create new quantum Betti numbers: for integer boundary matrices the ranks over Q and C agree.',
 'Compare the two degree patterns.',
 'K₂ gives curvature zero and K₃ positive curvature despite both being trivial finite decision inputs. The value describes transport geometry of the chosen graph, not a proved attack cost or a worst-case complexity class.',
 'A mathematically defined observable can be computed exactly while its intended predictive interpretation remains unsupported.',
 'No cryptographic vulnerability estimate or attack procedure is provided. The historical hash-state complexes, datasets and generalization claims were not recovered sufficiently for validation.',
 'Predefine graph construction and measures, then test a declared prediction theorem or held-out association while separating confounding degree effects from causal claims.')
add(33,'union-closed sets conjecture','A singleton gives an exact Frankl injection','Extremal set theory',
 'Which union-closed families admit a direct half-frequency proof, and what do small enumerations establish?',
 'The July2 project studied Frankl’s conjecture with topological summaries and empirical counts. A simple injection gives a genuine theorem for a broad subclass. Exhaustive small-universe checks can then be certified, while correlations and reported Betti values remain distinct evidence.',
 ['A finite family F of subsets is union-closed if A∪B∈F whenever A,B∈F. Frankl’s conjecture asks whether every nontrivial finite such family has an element belonging to at least half its sets. Assume the union of the family is nonempty.'],
 'Singleton certificate',
 'If{a}∈F, then a belongs to at least|F|/2 members of F. The map A↦A∪{a} injects the members not containing a into those containing a.',
 'Union closure puts A∪{a} in F. If A and B both omit a and their unions with{a} agree, removing a gives A=B, proving injectivity. Thus the number omitting a is at most the number containing it, exactly the desired inequality. This proof makes no use of a topology or an entropy. It also explains its limitation: a family with no singleton does not admit this particular injection automatically. A complete search on a fixed finite universe can test all families by exact unions and frequencies, but it cannot prove the unbounded conjecture. Reported small Betti vectors additionally require the actual associated complex and coefficient convention.',
 'Use F={∅,{1},{1,2},{1,3},{1,2,3}}.',
 'This family is union-closed and contains{1}. Four of its five sets contain1. The only set omitting1 is∅, whose injection image is{1}. A portable checker can independently enumerate every family on three labels and verify the frequency statement for that finite scope.',
 'The exact injection is a theorem; finite enumeration is a bounded certificate; neither is replaced by a topology–complexity correlation.',
 'No general solution of Frankl’s conjecture is claimed. The historical n=4,n=5 counts depend on an unrecovered critical-strip selection rule and are not reproduced as facts.',
 'Specify the selected family class and seek an explicit injection or compression argument that applies beyond the singleton case.')
add(34,'B_{12}=-691/2730','Bernoulli691 belongs to a precise coefficient identity','Special numbers and modular forms',
 'What is the exact Bernoulli coefficient, and which obstruction claims follow only by definition?',
 'The user corrected a fabricated Bernoulli value in the July6 exchange. The correction is exact and can be reproduced rationally. The proposed obstruction energy is then a deliberately weighted dimension, not a new arithmetic explanation of cohomology or Kervaire phenomena.',
 ['Define Bernoulli numbers by t/(e^t−1)=ΣB_nt^n/n!. For k≥2 use the normalized Eisenstein convention E_{2k}=1−(4k/B_{2k})Σ_{n≥1}σ_{2k−1}(n)q^n. The proposed E_obs(H^n)=dimH^n·|B_{2n+2}| is a stipulated functional.'],
 'The exact weight-twelve normalization',
 'B₁₂=−691/2730 and−24/B₁₂=65520/691, so E₁₂=1+(65520/691)Σσ₁₁(n)q^n. For a finite-dimensional H⁵, the stipulated energy is(691/2730)dimH⁵.',
 'Multiplying the defining Bernoulli series by e^t−1 and comparing coefficients yieldsΣ_{j=0}^n C(n+1,j)B_j=0 for n≥1, with B₀=1. This recursively computes B₁₂ exactly using rational arithmetic. Substitution into the declared Eisenstein normalization gives the coefficient. Substituting n=5 into the proposed energy gives its scalar multiple of dimension. It therefore vanishes precisely when H⁵ has zero dimension, but says nothing about whether a particular class in a nonzero cohomology group vanishes. The integer691 appears because the definition chose this Bernoulli weight; a canonical arithmetic obstruction requires a separately constructed map or theorem.',
 'Take dimH⁵=2.',
 'The chosen energy is691/1365. Replacing H⁵ by another two-dimensional vector space leaves the energy unchanged regardless of its origin or any distinguished class. Likewise the list2,6,14,30,62,126 is generated by2^(j+1)−2 for j=1,…,6; that arithmetic observation is not an existence theorem for Kervaire-invariant classes.',
 'The genuine modular coefficient is preserved while unsupported interpretations are kept separate.',
 'No Gödel,P-versus-NP,Kervaire or quantum-error-correction implication is proved. Nonzero cohomology dimension alone does not detect a chosen obstruction class.',
 'Construct the proposed arithmetic pairing on actual classes and prove its invariance before selecting a numerical weight.',[('NIST DLMF Bernoulli numbers','https://dlmf.nist.gov/24.2')])
add(35,'restriction/induction and double-coset','A Mackey decomposition can be proved by orbit splitting','Representation theory',
 'What concrete finite-group identity supports the named induction/restriction framework?',
 'The July4 material invoked spectral Mackey functors, chromatic theories and descent. The finite permutation case provides a complete classical Mackey calculation. It supplies an actual double-coset decomposition without pretending to construct the equivariant spectral machinery.',
 ['Let G=S₃ and H={1,(12)}. The induced trivial representation Ind_H^G1 is the permutation representation on the three left cosets G/H. Restrict it back to H and decompose by the H-orbits on those cosets.'],
 'An explicit double-coset decomposition',
 'Res_H^G Ind_H^G1≅1_H⊕Ind_{1}^{H}1. Over characteristic zero this is2·1_H⊕sign_H and has dimension three.',
 'One coset is H itself and is fixed by H. The other two cosets are exchanged by(12), so their permutation module is the regular representation of C₂. A fixed orbit contributes a trivial line; a free two-element orbit contributes Ind_1^H1. Equivalently H∖G/H has representatives1 and(13). The first intersection H∩H is H, and the second H∩(13)H(13)⁻¹ is trivial, giving exactly the two terms. The two-dimensional regular module splits into symmetric and antisymmetric lines by the vectors(1,1) and(1,−1). This proves the representation identity directly and illustrates the orbit argument underlying the general Mackey formula.',
 'Evaluate characters at the identity and at(12).',
 'The three-coset permutation character is(3,1): the identity fixes all cosets, and the transposition fixes one. The right side2·trivial+sign gives(3,2−1)=(3,1), matching the explicit orbit decomposition.',
 'The named correspondence now has defined groups, spaces and maps with a complete finite proof.',
 'This does not construct a functor between symmetric spectra and genuine G-spectra, prove Segal completion or identify a Morava-theoretic obstruction.',
 'Specify the equivariant category and its transfer/restriction maps, then verify the relevant double-coset and coherence identities rather than importing them from analogy.',[LIE])

